#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129451 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129236 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36b04804-a1f3-57de-b966-65074210fc6b" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Product or Quotient of Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a8bfb4dd-e556-5d6f-aaef-fddc9a598988" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126207} {:answer/id #uuid "bee69923-eae2-5ddf-a42b-d1b058dc8af9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126208} {:answer/id #uuid "db97e4bf-99f0-504e-a725-2a9b37da3c66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126209} {:answer/id #uuid "672fa625-0676-5877-87a0-a3ecd00928cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126210} {:answer/id #uuid "767cf28e-15b6-599c-8613-52500710d20b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126211}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8bfb4dd-e556-5d6f-aaef-fddc9a598988" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126207} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9b62cac-9385-5bff-8c35-d1b52ef42ebf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126206}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "18fad891-cd2e-5b5f-9e81-fba725a5c444" :question/math-academy-id "q-133430" :question/problem "Given that $a$ is an **irrational** number, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $17$ is rational\n\nII. $17a$ is rational\n\nIII. $a\\cdot 17$ is irrational" :question/worked-solution "Recall that: \n\n- the product of two rational numbers is *always* rational:\n\n$$\n\\text{rational}\\times \\text{rational}=\\text{rational}\n$$\n\n- the product of a nonzero rational number and an irrational number is *always* irrational:\n\n$$\n\\text{rational}\\times \\text{irrational}=\\text{irrational}\n$$\n\n- the product of two irrational numbers is *sometimes* rational and *sometimes* irrational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true.\n\n- Statement II is false. The product of a rational number ($17$) and an irrational number ($a$) is irrational.\n\n- Statement III is true. We know from part II that $17a$ is irrational. And since multiplication is commutative, we conclude that $a\\cdot 17$ is irrational.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518120 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129235 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbf0b9c3-84ba-51c8-bb7e-a45a441834ea" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Sum or Difference of Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4afb3715-3f88-56d5-b275-bc81df1d0871" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517633} {:answer/id #uuid "4bc3bc0f-8609-527a-aef7-251c33edf7a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517637} {:answer/id #uuid "592eee93-53ea-5ef8-a416-d9e903eec0f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517654} {:answer/id #uuid "6f10f982-4e9f-520e-8314-481be6073a3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517702} {:answer/id #uuid "7a8a4a04-79cb-50a8-82a9-14ab1f07c5bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517720}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4bc3bc0f-8609-527a-aef7-251c33edf7a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517637} :answer-field/id #uuid "bbb07e29-0dfc-5f19-8c86-10222960800a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518059}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2b569a4a-634a-5c5d-8e62-c4297125cda8" :question/math-academy-id "q-133670" :question/problem "Given that $a$ is an **irrational** number, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\frac{1}{7}$ is irrational\n\nII. $\\frac{1}{7}+a$ is always irrational\n\nIII. $a-\\frac{1}{7}$ is sometimes rational and sometimes irrational" :question/worked-solution "Recall that: \n\n- the sum of two rational numbers is *always* rational:\n\n$$\n\\text{rational}+\\text{rational}=\\text{rational}\n$$\n\n- the sum of a rational number and an irrational number is *always* irrational:\n\n$$\n\\text{rational}+\\text{irrational}=\\text{irrational}\n$$\n\n- the sum of two irrational numbers is *sometimes* rational and *sometimes* irrational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false.\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\frac{1}{7}$ is rational and $a$ is irrational, their sum is always an irrational number.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\frac{1}{7}$ is rational and $a$ is irrational, their difference is always an irrational number.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518210 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129235 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbf0b9c3-84ba-51c8-bb7e-a45a441834ea" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Sum or Difference of Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b1658d7-fb9d-5945-a721-88c70ad2282a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517510} {:answer/id #uuid "6968ee8d-91bd-5ab9-bb4d-43e24c86d97a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517681} {:answer/id #uuid "8638d565-4f58-5ed6-9bb5-a0a33a0508a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517740} {:answer/id #uuid "ba0a4dea-a87b-5f8a-a8c1-8a1b2cb1d7c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517839} {:answer/id #uuid "d3fa6e00-f1ae-53d5-ba58-bc05ad657bc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517886}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba0a4dea-a87b-5f8a-a8c1-8a1b2cb1d7c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517839} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b4cee67-86ab-5077-982d-73159c288590" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517978}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e5ec21fc-d95b-5a6e-8483-328c912290e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-133671" :question/problem "Given that $a$ is an **irrational** number, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $-\\frac{1}{{4}^{12}}$ is irrational\n\nII. $a-\\frac{1}{{4}^{12}}$ is always rational\n\nIII. $a+\\frac{1}{{4}^{12}}$ is sometimes rational and sometimes irrational" :question/worked-solution "Recall that: \n\n- the sum of two rational numbers is *always* rational:\n\n$$\n\\text{rational}+\\text{rational}=\\text{rational}\n$$\n\n- the sum of a rational number and an irrational number is *always* irrational:\n\n$$\n\\text{rational}+\\text{irrational}=\\text{irrational}\n$$\n\n- the sum of two irrational numbers is *sometimes* rational and *sometimes* irrational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false.\n\n- Statements II and III are false. Since $-\\frac{1}{{4}^{12}}$ is rational and $a$ is irrational, their sum or difference is always an irrational number.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""}]}
